WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.580
The early 2000s saw an explosion of forensic-based
procedural crime dramas, but Bones lasted

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:06.730
longer than most.

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:10.730
The series was focused on a team of forensic
anthropologists working at the Jeffersonian

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:15.970
Institute who lent their skills to the FBI
to take on mysterious bodies, serial killers,

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:16.970
and more.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:21.590
With a cast helmed by Emily Deschanel as Dr.
Temperance Brennan and David Boreanaz as FBI

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:26.320
Agent Seeley Booth, the show ran for 12 seasons
as the longest-running drama in Fox history.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.340
Even if you’ve seen every episode, the show
might still have a few mysteries to offer.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.550
Here are the things you might not know about
Bones.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.890
High turnover at the Jeffersonian

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:39.990
Any show that runs for 12 seasons is going
to change up its cast a little bit, but on

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:42.330
Bones, characters really knew how to make
an exit.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:46.350
The third season was capped off with the shocking
reveal that fan-favorite character Zack Addy

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:50.390
was secretly the apprentice of a cannibalistic
serial killer that Brennan and Booth had been

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:51.650
hunting all year.

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:55.150
One character’s departure from the show,
however, had more to do with real life than

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:57.190
the story unfolding at the Jeffersonian.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:01.880
FBI psychologist Lance Sweets, played by John
Francis Daley for seven years, was killed

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:06.310
off in the season 10 premiere as the victim
of a conspiracy surrounding Agent Booth.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:07.310
The real-life reason?

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:11.350
When he isn’t acting, Daley has a prolific
career as a screenwriter with writing partner

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:15.450
Jonathan Goldstein, producing the scripts
for plenty of movies, including Spider-Man:

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:16.450
Homecoming.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:21.350
In 2014, they signed on to write and direct
the remake of National Lampoon's Vacation.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:25.880
Daley needed four months off for the project,
and rather than sending Sweets on a long vacation,

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.800
the show’s writers decided it would be simpler
to just kill him off.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.690
Legal trouble behind the camera

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:35.670
It might not be as dramatic as digging up
a dead body or tracking down a serial killer,

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:39.300
but Bones had its share of legal trouble behind
the scenes, too.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:44.170
In 2015, executive producer Barry Josephson
sued Fox for withholding profits from a number

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:47.799
of people including stars Emily Deschanel
and David Boreanaz.

00:01:47.799 --> 00:01:51.950
The suit claimed they were owed as much as
24 million dollars, accrued from airing the

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.510
show in Europe, online streaming, and product
placement deals.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:59.490
Despite the suit, Fox still renewed the show
for its 11th and 12th seasons, while the case

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.409
went into arbitration.

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:04.240
The real forensic expert: Kathy Reichs

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.220
In the early 2000s, Fox asked writer/producer
Hart Hanson to create a forensics-based crime

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:13.409
show not unlike CBS's extremely popular CSI
franchise.

00:02:13.409 --> 00:02:18.329
After meeting with producer Barry Josephson,
Hanson watched a documentary about forensic

00:02:18.329 --> 00:02:20.689
anthropologist and crime novelist Kathy Reichs,
later saying

00:02:20.689 --> 00:02:23.249
“What she could do with a pile of bones
was amazing."

00:02:23.249 --> 00:02:27.360
The result was Bones, which would approach
the science-based procedural from a different

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.360
angle, with a little more humor and a lot
more skeletons.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.319
Rather than base it on Reichs herself, though,
the show would focus on Temperance Brennan,

00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:36.909
the forensic anthropologist from Reichs's
novels.

00:02:36.909 --> 00:02:41.170
As for Reichs herself, she considers Bones
to be less of an adaptation and more of a

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:44.970
prequel, since Emily Deschanel was over a
decade younger than Brennan was in the first

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:46.019
book.

00:02:46.019 --> 00:02:47.519
The Finder

00:02:47.519 --> 00:02:50.959
Season six brought viewers an episode called
"The Finder," which introduced Geoff Stults

00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:55.069
as Major Walter Sherman, an Iraq War veteran
whose brain injury left him with the keen

00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:58.670
ability to see connections in things most
ordinary people could not.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:02.819
Michael Clarke Duncan played Leo Knox, an
attorney turned bar owner and Walter's sidekick

00:03:02.819 --> 00:03:04.140
in his detective duties.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:05.379
“He's not crazy.

00:03:05.379 --> 00:03:10.299
Where I come from we call Walter's gift Darna
Hula the second sight.”

00:03:10.299 --> 00:03:14.650
Nine months after those characters were introduced
on Bones, The Finder debuted on Fox.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:18.730
Unfortunately, audiences seemed to like forensic
investigations a little more than brain injuries

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.169
that give you crime-fighting super-powers.

00:03:21.169 --> 00:03:25.669
Rather than racking up another 248-episode
run like Bones, The Finder was canceled after

00:03:25.669 --> 00:03:26.889
only 13.

00:03:26.889 --> 00:03:30.529
There were plans for the characters to return
on Bones, but sadly, Duncan suffered a fatal

00:03:30.529 --> 00:03:35.170
heart attack a few months after The Finder
wrapped up, ending any further plans for a

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:36.170
return.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:38.089
The layover that became a role

00:03:38.089 --> 00:03:41.879
Bones enjoyed quite a few notable guest stars
over the years, including Emily Deschanel's

00:03:41.879 --> 00:03:46.589
real-life sister Zooey Deschanel and the surprising
reveal that Angela’s dad is actually Billy

00:03:46.589 --> 00:03:48.200
Gibbons from ZZ Top.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:52.819
For British actor, writer, quiz show host
and all-around bon-vivant Stephen Fry, however,

00:03:52.819 --> 00:03:56.989
what started out as a desire to grab lunch
with a friend on a layover wound up turning

00:03:56.989 --> 00:04:00.260
into a recurring role on one of TV’s most
popular shows.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:04.900
In 2007, Fry was on his way to New Zealand
to meet with Lord of the Rings director Peter

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.620
Jackson, and decided to break up the long
trip with a stop in Los Angeles.

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:12.109
Since he was in the neighborhood anyway, Fry’s
agent pitched him on doing Bones, and while

00:04:12.109 --> 00:04:16.310
Fry had never heard of it, he mentioned it
to his friend and long-time collaborator Hugh

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:17.310
Laurie.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:20.310
Laurie was starring in House, which was shot
on the same lot, and had good things to say

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.130
about the cast and crew of Bones.

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:24.870
"I said what do you know about these Bones
and he said well they're rather nice they're

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.420
just around the corner on the fox lot"

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:30.970
With that, Fry signed on to play Gordon Wyatt,
Booth’s psychologist.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:34.870
He eventually appeared in six episodes of
the show, and presumably grabbed lunch with

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:36.880
Laurie a few times while he was at it.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:38.890
Dodging the cancellation bullet

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:42.790
If you watched the last episode of Season
10 and thought that it felt an awful lot like

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:45.060
a series finale, there’s a reason for that.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.420
When the episode was made, Fox executives
hadn’t yet decided to renew the show.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:53.280
That put showrunner Stephen Nathan in the
position of having to switch things up.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.200
Rather than ending the series on a cliffhanger
like he’d intended, “The Next in the Last”

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.050
had to be an episode that could work as a
satisfying ending for a long-running series,

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:05.350
while still leaving the door open for its
possible return if they did get picked up.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:07.450
“Oh, I’m done.”

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:11.230
The result was an episode built around themes
of transition and reflection, where Brennan

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:14.480
and Booth considered moving on from their
work at the Jeffersonian.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.240
Needless to say, they didn’t have to.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.680
Bones was picked up for another two seasons
before finally wrapping up in 2017.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.900
And with an episode title like “The End
in the End,” they were definitely sure they

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:26.140
weren’t coming back this time.

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.070
The explosive finale

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:32.760
After 12 years, Bones literally went out with
a bang: the Jeffersonian facilities were destroyed,

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:36.550
and it was an explosion that had been building
since the very first episode.

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:40.680
During the pilot, creator Hart Hanson had
a conversation with David Boreanaz in which

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.870
Boreanaz said “I want to blow up this lab.”

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:47.230
Hanson agreed that one day, he could, and
while he would later leave the show, his successor

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:48.600
kept his promise.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:52.450
Not only did they blow up the lab, but as
the director of the final episode, Boreanaz

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:53.410
was the one who got to do it.

